    body: `
<p>Directions in Taigi use a handful of words, and one habit you already know from the lesson on places: <b>where</b> comes before the action.</p>
<h3>Right and left</h3>
<p>Taigi names the sides after your hands. <b>正手</b> <i>tsiànn-tshiú</i> is your right hand and <b>倒手</b> <i>tò-tshiú</i> your left hand. For the side of something, the words are <b>正爿</b> <i>tsiànn-pîng</i> (the right side) and <b>倒爿</b> <i>tò-pîng</i> (the left side).</p>
{{ex:伊寫字、食飯攏用倒手。|I siá-jī, tsia̍h-pn̄g lóng īng tò-tshiú.|He writes and eats with his left hand.}}
<h3>Go straight</h3>
<p><b>直直</b> <i>ti̍t-ti̍t</i> means "straight on" and <b>行</b> <i>kiânn</i> means "walk, go". Put them together, in that order, and you have the most useful direction of all.</p>
{{ex:直直行。|Ti̍t-ti̍t kiânn.|Go straight ahead.}}
{{ex:直直行就到矣。|Ti̍t-ti̍t kiânn tō kàu--ah.|Go straight and you're there.}}
<p><b>就到矣</b> <i>tō kàu--ah</i> at the end means "and then you're there": <b>到</b> <i>kàu</i> is "arrive", and 矣 says the change has happened.</p>
<h3>Turn at the intersection</h3>
<p>The verb for turning is <b>斡</b> <i>uat</i>. The side comes right after it: <b>斡正爿</b> (turn right), <b>斡倒爿</b> (turn left). An intersection is <b>路口</b> <i>lōo-kháu</i>.</p>
<p>English says "turn right <i>at the intersection</i>", with the place at the end. Taigi says the place first, then the turn: <b>路口斡正爿</b>, literally "intersection, turn right". This is the same rule as with 佇: the place goes before the verb.</p>
{{ex:頭前路口斡正爿就到矣。|Thâu-tsîng lōo-kháu uat tsiànn-pîng tō kàu--ah.|Turn right at the intersection ahead and you're there.}}
{{ex:頭前路口斡倒爿。|Thâu-tsîng lōo-kháu uat tò-pîng.|Turn left at the intersection ahead.}}
<p><b>頭前</b> <i>thâu-tsîng</i> here means "ahead, in front", so <b>頭前路口</b> is "the intersection up ahead". Its opposite is <b>後壁</b> <i>āu-piah</i>, "behind, at the back".</p>
<h3>Asking the way</h3>
<p>To ask where something is, use the pattern from the places lesson, thing + 佇 + place, and put <b>佗位</b> <i>tó-uī</i> (where) in the place slot. The question word stays where the answer will go.</p>
{{ex:便所佇佗位？|Piān-sóo tī tó-uī?|Where is the restroom?}}
<p>Put together, a full set of directions is a chain of short steps, each with the place first: go straight, then at the intersection ahead turn right, and you are there. Read the example sentences again with that in mind.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> putting the place at the end, as in English. <b>斡正爿路口</b> → <b>路口斡正爿</b>. Say where first, then what to do there.</div>
`,
