    id: "negation",
    title: "Four words for not: 毋, 無, 袂, 莫",
    blurb: "Choose the right 'not' every time: 毋, 無, 袂, 莫 and 免, side by side.",
    deck: "Traps for English speakers",
    body: `
<p>English has one "not" and puts it anywhere. Taigi has four everyday negatives, plus a fifth for "no need", and each one carries its own meaning. You have met them one by one; here they are together.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td class="k">毋</td><td>isn't; won't (refuse)</td><td class="k">毋是</td></tr><tr><td class="k">無</td><td>doesn't have; didn't</td><td class="k">無食</td></tr><tr><td class="k">袂</td><td>can't; won't happen</td><td class="k">袂曉</td></tr><tr><td class="k">莫</td><td>don't! (telling someone)</td><td class="k">莫買</td></tr><tr><td class="k">免</td><td>no need</td><td class="k">免來</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>毋: isn't, won't</h3>
<p><b>毋</b> <i>m̄</i> negates 是 and also says you refuse: <b>毋是</b>, isn't. You also know it from <b>毋知</b>, I don't know. Because it can sound like "I won't", it is the wrong choice for things that simply did not happen.</p>
{{ex:伊毋是老師。|I m̄ sī lāu-su.|He isn't a teacher.}}
<h3>無: doesn't have, didn't</h3>
<p><b>無</b> <i>bô</i> is the negative of 有, so it covers "don't have" and "didn't do". English "I didn't eat" uses "not"; Taigi uses 無.</p>
{{ex:我無食早起。|Guá bô tsia̍h tsá-khí.|I didn't eat breakfast.}}
<p>For "don't want", Taigi says <b>無愛</b> <i>bô ài</i>. 毋 there would sound like a flat refusal.</p>
{{ex:我無愛。|Guá bô ài.|I don't want it.}}
<h3>袂: can't, won't happen</h3>
<p><b>袂</b> <i>bē</i> is the negative of the 會 words from the last lesson: 袂 (won't happen), 袂曉 (don't know how), 袂使 (not allowed), 袂當 (not able). Whenever the positive has 會, the negative has 袂.</p>
{{ex:我袂曉。|Guá bē-hiáu.|I don't know how.}}
<h3>莫: don't!</h3>
<p><b>莫</b> <i>mài</i> tells someone not to do something. It is the word for warnings and advice, and it often comes with 啦 at the end to soften or urge, as in <b>莫買啦</b>.</p>
{{ex:叫你莫來你閣來。|Kiò lí mài lâi lí koh lâi.|I told you not to come, and you came anyway.}}
<h3>免: no need</h3>
<p><b>免</b> <i>bián</i> means it isn't necessary, and <b>毋免</b> <i>m̄-bián</i> says the same thing. On its own, <b>免啦！</b> is the MOE's example for "no need!" (不用啦).</p>
{{ex:你免來。|Lí bián lâi.|You don't need to come.}}
{{ex:免啦！|Bián--lah!|No need!}}
<h3>Where the negative goes</h3>
<p>The negative sits right before the verb, after any time word: <b>我明仔載無愛去</b>, I don't want to go tomorrow. Classifiers and 兩 stay as they are: <b>我無買兩本冊</b>.</p>
<p>A quick test before you speak: is it about being something (毋), having or doing (無), being able (袂), telling someone (莫) or necessity (免)? Answer that, and the word picks itself.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> using 毋 for everything, like English "not". <b>我毋食早起</b> → <b>我無食早起</b> for "I didn't eat breakfast".</div>
`,
